[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pdf-not-opening\",\n    \"title\": \"How to Fix Common PDF Opening Errors: Complete Guide\",\n    \"description\": \"Solve PDF loading and opening errors. Understand damaged headers, file format corruptions, password restrictions, and how to repair them.\",\n    \"keywords\": [\"pdf not opening\", \"fix pdf error\", \"corrupted pdf\", \"pdf reader error\", \"open pdf online\"],\n    \"category\": \"pdf\",\n    \"readTime\": \"6 min read\",\n    \"sections\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Introduction to PDF Opening Failures\",\n        \"content\": \"Encountering a PDF that refuses to open can halt business tasks. When a browser, Adobe Acrobat, or mobile document viewer displays errors like 'Format error: Not a PDF or corrupted', or requests decryption passwords you do not have, finding the root cause is essential. Rather than re-requesting or re-scanning the files, understanding how PDF file signatures and object catalogs operate lets you troubleshoot, repair, and unlock documents safely.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"The Technical Root Causes of PDF Corruption\",\n        \"content\": \"PDF opening errors are rarely random; they usually result from structural inconsistencies. The most common cause is a Damaged File Header. A standard PDF must begin with a version identifier like %PDF-1.7. If a download is cut short, this header can be truncated. Another major cause is Broken Cross-Reference Tables (xref). The xref table maps the exact byte offsets of all page objects. If a single byte is shifted, the reader cannot locate the object tree, resulting in rendering failure. Finally, File System Compression or incorrect email encoding can strip the EOF (End-of-File) marker %%EOF, causing readers to reject the document.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Step-by-Step Guide to Resolve PDF Errors\",\n        \"items\": [\n          \"Verify the File Header: Open the PDF in a plain text editor. Ensure the first line starts with %PDF- followed by the version number.\",\n